# Principal DDD Review — Cross-Domain Digitalisation Platform

Review date: 11 October 2026 · Evidence basis: the latest attached Claude execution transcript, including its status updates through approximately 08:16.

## 1. My conclusion: what are we actually doing?

We are transforming a collection of legacy application models and partially implemented domain components into a coherent, secure, historically correct, cross-domain platform for organisations and political parties.

The objective is not simply to introduce DDD patterns or produce architecture documents. It is to establish authoritative domain facts and reliable relationships between:

- Organisation and Member/Membership — who belongs to which organisation, and when.
- Committee, Position and Tenure — who holds which position, within which committee and for what period.
- Authorization and Permission — which actions an actor may perform, based on authoritative facts and explicit policies.
- Geography and Jurisdiction — which geographical units exist, how they relate, and which committees are responsible for which areas.
- Discussion Forum/Communication — who may read, post, moderate or manage discussions.
- Audit and Decision Evidence — why a protected action was allowed or denied, and whether that decision can be reconstructed later.

Election workflows remain out of scope.

The latest evidence supports a clear distinction: the architecture research is advanced, but the integrated application is not yet demonstrated. Membership has a tested kernel; Governance and Geography have significant implementation gaps; and the authorization and forum design has not yet been proven end to end.&#x20;

