## 3. Principal findings by bounded context

| Context              | Principal finding                                                                                    | Required architectural response                                                                                  |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Membership           | Tested core, but only Admit/End and not the live source of truth                                     | Preserve the ratified domain rules; implement integration ports, transaction handling and controlled cutover     |
| Committee/Governance | Legacy representations remain; authorization, removal and historical-integrity defects are reported  | Contain security issues, freeze legacy behaviour where authorized, then implement Governance with reconciliation |
| Geography            | Tenant isolation and historical integrity are insufficient                                           | Fix organisation scoping and path consistency; establish revisioned history and a Geography query contract       |
| Authorization        | Formal models and vectors exist, but no integrated policy decision point has been demonstrated       | Ratify semantics, implement deterministic policy evaluation and test it against authoritative facts              |
| Forum                | Target design only; proposed first vertical slice is not yet proven                                  | Build one test-only protected command after its contracts and prerequisites are approved                         |
| Audit/replay         | Research supports version-based reconstruction, but the application lacks complete decision evidence | Define the DecisionRecord schema and prove atomic writes and reproducible decisions                              |

These are not six independent implementation projects. Their dependencies matter: Authorization depends on facts owned by other contexts; Geography must be trustworthy before it supports jurisdiction decisions; and Forum is a useful first consumer because it tests several boundaries together.

## 4. What should we do now?

My recommendation is to run three tightly controlled workstreams in parallel, rather than expand the architecture corpus further.

## Workstream A — Security containment

Priority 0

Provision the isolated test database with reviewed setup evidence, then authorize and execute the security investigation. Write failing tests first for committee write authorization, self-approval, member export and cross-organisation Geography access. Include the global-role and debug-route findings once their backlog items are authorized.

Exit condition: negative authorization tests and tenant-isolation tests pass, affected write routes are guarded, and an independent reviewer verifies the evidence.

## Workstream B — Close the verification round

Bounded research

Recover the actual E9 process state and output. Complete the PARENT_TOKEN branch or record why it cannot complete. Update the closure document and model README only with measured results.

Exit condition: each experiment has a reproducible script/version, exact counts, limitations and a clear pass/fail interpretation. E9 stays open until that evidence exists.

## Workstream C — Obtain architecture decisions

PO / ARB

Submit the prepared decision sheet, especially DR-07 (Committee ownership), the shared-database assumption, Authorization semantics, ScopeToken ownership, DO versus PO, and Geography versioning.

Exit condition: every decision has an authorized outcome, rationale and recorded consequences. Recommendations remain proposals until then.

The isolated test database is the most important shared infrastructure dependency because it unblocks the security investigation and later database-backed integration tests. The PO/ARB decision round can proceed in parallel.&#x20;

